 :adr/title "Two of AIUEOS K16's five blocker classes were closable here: 202 GB of runaway tmp output, and a bigint in ELF packaging that phase-bisection could not reach"
 :adr/status "accepted"
 :adr/date "2026-09-10"
 :adr/superseded-by nil
 :adr/related ["adr-2608136000-eight-questions" "adr-2609051700-foundation-library-constant-factors"]
 :adr/body "Four of AIUEOS K16's five blocker classes were recorded as needing someone
else -- a credential, a board, an artifact, a firmware -- plus one compiler
bug that three measurements had concluded a pin advance would not fix. Two of
those five turned out to be closable here, and this ADR records how, because
in both cases the thing that unblocked them was a control that had not been
run rather than work that had not been done.

## The disk was 202 GB of runaway command output

`/private/tmp` held 213 GB across 69,301 entries. Twenty files were over
200 MB and accounted for ~202 GB of it: an 80 GB captured `lsof` listing, a
30 GB file of one repeated `WARNING: Use of :paths external to the project`,
a 22 GB and a 14 GB grep of ledger text, and so on down. Every one was a
command whose output was redirected to a file and whose input was larger than
the author expected. None was open by any process (`lsof` over all twenty
returned nothing) and none had been touched since 2026-09-09.

Removed: **14 GB free at 99% -> 204 GB free at 79%**, measured with `df`.

This mattered to coverage because seven `:model-artifact` markers needed a
10.9 GB GGUF and the coverage contract had recorded, correctly, that the
fetch was `a decision about the owner's disk, not a coverage decision`. With
the disk answered the fetch is ordinary: the repository's own pinned
downloader returned `AIUEOS_QWEN38_ARTIFACT_OK` with the byte count and
SHA-256 both matching the pin.

**The class is worth naming: a redirected command that produces more than
expected leaves no error, no log line and no owner.** It is discovered by
size, days later, by someone looking for something else. The cheap guard is
to bound the producer rather than the consumer -- `head -c`, a `LIMIT`, a
`--max-count` -- and the cheap detection is `find <dir> -size +200M`, which
answers in seconds where `du` on 69,301 entries does not answer at all.

## The compiler bug was in packaging, and targets found it where phases could not

`(cap-call 16 0)` compiled for `x86_64-aiueos-user-v1` exited 70 with
`:kotoba/internal-error`. Two earlier sessions had reached the real exception
-- `TypeError: Cannot mix BigInt and other types` -- by defeating amu's
`error-report`, which reads `(ex-data error)`, finds no `:phase`, defaults to
`:internal` and discards the message. Both then bisected by PHASE: `check`
ok, `--target js` ok, native fails, therefore x86_64 code generation. That
sent the reader to `x86_64.cljc`'s `emit-cap-call`, which already coerces its
capability id with `js/Number` and carries a comment naming this exact error.
The trail ended there for a day.

**Bisecting by neighbouring TARGET ends it in one command.** The same source
compiles on `wasm32`, `x86_64-linux`, `aarch64-macos`,
`x86_64-aiueos-kernel-v1`, `aarch64-aiueos-kernel-v1` and
`x86_64-aiueos-uefi-v1`, and `x86_64-aiueos-user-v1` compiles fine without
`cap-call`. One target, one form, and only their intersection. That names a
route, not a phase: `package-user`, whose sole arithmetic on a capability id
is `capability-bitmap` in kotoba-native's `elf64.cljc` --
`(quot id 8)` and `(mod id 8)` against an id that is a bigint on cljs.

**A failing case tells you what broke; only a passing neighbour tells you
where.** Six passing neighbours were one command away throughout.

## The test that existed could not have caught it

`elf64_portable_test` already had `packs-a-user-image`. Its artifact declares
no effects, so the `reduce` inside `capability-bitmap` ran zero times: a user
image with no capabilities was proving the capability path. The added test
hands in a real bigint and asserts the bitmap BYTE across two ids sharing a
bitmap index (16 and 17 -> 1 and 2), because a coercion that silently lost
the value would also not throw. Control, run both ways on the same tree:
without the fix the suite exits 1 with the TypeError at `elf64.cljc:783`
inside `quot` inside `capability-bitmap`; with it, 68 tests / 638 assertions
/ 0 failures.

## The fix has to cross three pins, and only two of them have a gate

kotoba-native `b88a11b` -> main `5f2717c`. West pin advanced
`50c6480 -> 5f2717c` (root `eb5006c2`), which `verify-west-pins` covers. But
**amu pins kotoba-native by `:git/sha` in `deps.edn`, and there is no gate on
those** -- exactly the gap CLAUDE.md's foundation-library section names. So
the fix does not reach any `amu compile` until `kotoba-lang/amu#921` lands,
and amu's `main` requires a pull request and passing checks, so it cannot be
merged server-side the way the other two were.

## What it bought

`AIUEOS_PLC_NATIVE_BUILD_OK` with the build and its reproduction
byte-identical, and then the kernel printing
`AIUEOS_PLC_RT_OK ... capabilities=16,17,18,19` -- the ids that were
crashing. realtime 1/1. A second, smaller gap closed on the way:
`smoke-qemu-uefi.sh` defaulted `AIUEOS_PLC_ELF` to `build/plc-motor/`, a
directory nothing in the tree writes, so the documented profile could not run
unaided even with a working compiler.

**The headline did not move.** CORE stays 91/116 78.4%; realtime is an
excluded row. Saying that first is the point of the entry.

## What is not settled

The PLC gate is intermittent: 3 green of 7 runs, failing
`AIUEOS_PLC_RT_FAIL embedded-elf-provider-or-safe-state loader=7` with
`stage=` 92, 25, 4 and 19 -- a different stage each time, which is the shape
of a timing failure and not of a wrong path. Host load1 was 26-42 with a
second QEMU running throughout, and the profile it asserts is
`release=apic-absolute-ticks cycle=10ticks` with `deadline` among its failure
modes. A loaded host is the first hypothesis and it has NOT been tested on a
quiet one. The marker is reachable and has been emitted; the gate is not yet
known to be reliable.

Separately, amu's `error-report` discarding the message of an exception that
carries no `:phase` remains true and unfixed. It is what made a one-line
TypeError read as an unactionable internal error for a day, and it is a
smaller change than anything else in this entry.

## Resume points

1. `kotoba-lang/amu#921` -- merge when checks pass. Until then no `amu
   compile` sees the fix.
2. Run the PLC gate on an idle machine and settle whether the flakiness is
   the gate's or the host's.
3. The seven `:model-artifact` markers now need their gates run; the artifact
   and the disk are no longer in the way.
4. Fix `error-report` so an exception with no `:phase` keeps its message.
5. Still owner-side: the Murakumo service token plus install intent (9
   markers), powering the K16 (8), a TPM-enabled OVMF (1)."}]
